## How Acrylic Application Can Weaken Your Natural Nails

Picture the appointment itself. Your tech buffs the surface of your natural nail to rough it up slightly, giving the acrylic something to grip. Done correctly, that's a light pass, just enough to dull the shine. Done poorly, the file keeps going past the surface and into the nail plate, thinning it before any product touches it.

That over-filing is where application-stage damage starts. A nail plate is layers of keratin, stacked like thin sheets. Filing too deep removes some of those layers for good, and the nail won't grow them back until the whole plate cycles out from the base, which is why one heavy-handed appointment can stay visible for months.

Chemistry matters too. Salon-grade acrylic uses ethyl methacrylate (EMA), a monomer considered safe for nail use. Methyl methacrylate (MMA) is a cheaper monomer restricted in several regions, because it bonds too aggressively and is more likely to cause lifting and allergic reactions.

Whether <strong>do acrylic nails damage your nails</strong> at this stage comes down to one variable: how much of the plate got filed away before the product went on. A tech who files lightly and doesn't rush the base coat isn't putting your nails at real risk. <a href='/blog/acrylic-nails/how-to-do-acrylic-nails-at-home'>Avoiding nail damage during application</a> breaks down what conservative filing looks like.

