## Why Removal Is Usually Where the Real Damage Happens

Here's the part almost nobody warns you about clearly enough: removal causes more damage than application, and it isn't close. Prying a set off with a cuticle pusher, credit card, or dental floss, a method that circulates constantly online, doesn't just remove the acrylic. It rips away whatever layers of natural nail have bonded to it, sometimes several sheets at once.

That's how you end up with onycholysis, the nail plate separating from the nail bed underneath. It happens when force pulls the nail away faster than the bed can tolerate, leaving a pale patch where the two have come apart. It isn't usually painful, but it doesn't resolve until the section grows out.

A proper acetone soak avoids nearly all of this. Acetone dissolves the acrylic's bond gradually, so the product lifts away on its own rather than taking your nail with it. It can be drying, a real but minor trade-off next to the mechanical damage a dry pry causes. Fifteen to twenty minutes of soaking will always cost you less nail than a five-minute pry, even if it feels slower. The <a href='/blog/acrylic-nails/how-to-remove-acrylic-nails-at-home'>safe acrylic nail removal steps</a> guide walks through the full soak-off method.

