## How to Get Acrylics Without Damaging Your Nails

Picture a different version of that same appointment. Your tech files lightly, just enough to remove shine, and stops. Three to four weeks later, you're back for a fill rather than a full reapplication, which means less filing overall. Every few sets, you take a short break, two to three weeks, before starting again. That's the entire prevention model.

A few specifics worth asking for: a conservative file rather than a "sanded down" finish, EMA-based product rather than MMA, and soak off rather than pick or pry, even if it takes longer. Build in a break after every two to three fill cycles, roughly six to eight weeks, so the plate gets a real chance to rehydrate before any thinning compounds.

None of this demands perfection. One heavy-handed appointment won't undo years of healthy nails. What it does is stack the odds in your favour, so the acrylic stays exactly what it should be: a shell on top of a nail that's still doing fine underneath.

